[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: dynamic routing (FRRouting/RIP) on router2, not the physical WAN. After maintenance, FRR on router2 is stopped, so router2 no longer learns any 192.168.x.x routes.

Mechanism: Hosts in the Server Room (1.0.0.0/8) and Internet Lab (128.168.0.0/16) forward off-subnet traffic to router2. Because router2 lacks routes to 192.168.x.x, it immediately generates ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable,” which we observe when pinging 192.168.3.2: “From 1.0.0.1 … Net Unreachable” (pc1) and “From 128.168.0.1 … Net Unreachable” (pc2). Intra-zone traffic remains fine, matching the report.

Localization: router2’s control plane. Evidence:
- router2 routing table has only connected networks: 1.0.0.0/8 (eth0), 128.168.0.0/16 (eth1), 20.0.0.0/8 (eth2); no 192.168.x.x routes.
- FRR on router2 is inactive (dead) and its running config is empty.
- Physical WAN link to router1 is healthy: router2 eth2 (20.20.0.2/8) and router1 eth3 (20.20.0.1/8) are UP/LOWER_UP with “Link detected: yes” on both ends.

Root cause: FRR service on router2 was left stopped after maintenance, causing loss of dynamic routes to all 192.168.x.x networks.

Corrective fix: Restart and enable FRR on router2, restoring the RIP (or intended IGP) configuration to advertise 1.0.0.0/8 and 128.168.0.0/16 and learn 192.168.x.x networks. Verify routes appear on router2 and pings between 1.0.0.x/128.168.0.x and 192.168.x.x succeed. No WAN interface is down; router2’s WAN eth2 is up. === END ===